  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n- Rain Showers Today\n\nA shortwave trough is currently moving into the area bringing\nscattered rain showers. Most unstable CAPE values will increase\ninto this morning around a couple hundred J/kg. Therefore can't\nrule out some isolated thunder. Ahead of the cold front, southwest\nwinds will pick up with gusts around 25 mph this morning and early\nafternoon. As the front moves through late this morning and\nafternoon skies will clear with lighter northwest winds.\n\nTemperatures tonight will drop into the 30s with a chance for\ntemperatures below freezing along and north of US-10. Friday dry\nconditions are expected with highs only in the mid 50s to low 60s.\n\n- Slight Chances (20 to 30 percent) for Rain This Weekend\n\nA subtle feature may clip southwest Michigan Saturday bringing\nsome slight chances (20 to 30 percent) for rain. What ensemble\nmembers show rain keep any amounts light (less than a tenth of an\ninch). Timing wise, rain (if it occurs) looks to be during the\nmorning and afternoon.\n\nSunday is trending drier in the NBM with questionable low level\nmoisture and better mid level moisture. Will still be on the\nlookout for some showers, but dry air toward the surface may delay\nany additional rainfall.\n\n- Unsettled Pattern Next Week\n\nTaking another look at the long range ensembles and cluster\nanalysis, there continues to be differences in the overall\nsynoptic pattern next week. Monday is trending drier, with more\nmembers focusing on Tuesday for rainfall. Chances then continue\nthrough the rest of the week with trough moving through the\nPlains. Chances will be tied to the timing of this feature and how\nit evolves. This trough will move onshore Sunday and with better\nsampling more details should become clearer.\n\nWithin the largely southerly flow next week temperatures will\nremain mild for this time of year with highs in the 60s and lows \nin the 40s.",
